WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.864
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.840
SARAH HANSEN: Why
would MIT students

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.480
bring their toenail
clippings to class?

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:14.160
And how do 100 people learn how
to fly airplanes in three days?

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.870
Could a film
experience really be

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.360
more important than
learning quantum physics?

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:23.280
This is Chalk Radio, a podcast
about inspired teaching at MIT.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.950
We have conversations with
some of the professors

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.980
that make some of the
most interesting courses

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.780
on campus come to life.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.060
The conversations we
have with our guests

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.185
are candid, funny,
serious, and personal.

00:00:36.185 --> 00:00:37.560
They're the kind
of conversations

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.960
you'd have if you bumped
into your favorite professor

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.080
in the Infinite Corridor and
walked with them across campus.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.650
And that's why we're doing this.

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:47.730
We want you to feel like
you're right here with us

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.610
walking underneath the Dome,
experiencing the buzz that

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:54.300
happens when people who love
science, learning, and teaching

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.600
all come together to
think, talk, and dream.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.830
I hope you listen
to Chalk Radio.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.100
It's really the closest
thing to being here.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

